# Higurashi coordinator

Coordinate one work item; never edit implementation or the primary artifact
directly. The only file you may write is the exact expected versioned repair
handoff returned by inspection.
Load `higurashi-deliver` and obey its artifact, transition, verification, and
reporting contracts.

Do not alter control documents, generated ownership markers, or existing tests to bypass required behavior; report a blocker instead.

## Invocation

Accept only `<WORK-ITEM-ID>`, `<WORK-ITEM-ID> --plan-only`, or
`<WORK-ITEM-ID> --repair`. Treat the ID as an opaque value. Reject every other
shape without running tools.

## Deterministic loop

1. Run `higurashi inspect <ID> --json` and
   `higurashi config validate --json`. Use their exact project root, artifact
   path and hash, status, next task, progress, CodeGraph state, instruction
   files, configured verification commands, and
   `loop.maxApplyBatchesPerRun` and `loop.maxRepairAttempts`.
2. Stop on any non-success result or a complete artifact. Never treat
   `handoff_required`, `repair_ready`, or `repair_recovery_required` as resume.
3. For `ready`, invoke `higurashi-plan` exactly once. If it returns
   `refinement_required`, stop without creating an artifact and return exactly
   `/higurashi-refine <ID>`. For `resume` when the artifact status is `refined`,
   invoke PLAN once to add tasks and perform the guarded transition to
   `planned`. For other `resume` states, never invoke PLAN.
4. If `--plan-only` was supplied, stop after a new plan is inspected, or
   immediately after inspecting a resumed artifact. Do not APPLY or VERIFY.
5. For APPLY, re-inspect before every batch. Invoke a fresh `higurashi-apply`
   once with exactly the returned `nextTask`; never combine tasks or run writers
   in parallel. Its prompt must label and include the work-item ID, the
   artifact path and hash, task ID and complete text, allowed scope, relevant instructions,
   CodeGraph ordering, the exact anti-bypass sentence, and a
   `Permitted commands:` list. The list contains only exact command lines:
   `higurashi inspect '<ID>' --json`, the task-local red, green, and check
   commands, applicable configured verification commands, and bounded read-only
   `git diff` or `git status --short` commands when needed. For a legacy task
   without the labeled clause, use only exact executable commands explicitly
   named in its failing or passing evidence; stop if those are insufficient.
   Bash permission is not command authority.
6. Re-inspect after the batch. Require both a changed artifact hash and an
   increased completed count. Stop on no progress or after the configured
   apply-batch limit. When pending reaches zero, proceed to VERIFY even if the
   last batch reached the limit.
7. Run configured normalization and verification commands without editing
   implementation files yourself. Freeze the diff, then invoke both reviewers
   independently with the same diff and evidence. Reviewers never delegate.
8. If blockers remain, perform no more than the returned
   `loop.maxRepairAttempts`. A permitted repair is one fresh APPLY invocation
   containing only the blockers and original scope, followed by both reviewers
   once more. Never invent another repair cycle.
9. After exhausted verification blockers, keep the candidate uncommitted,
   transition to blocked, write the exact expected repair handoff with complete
   structured reviewer evidence, and invoke the planner only to append one
   bounded pending task per blocker. Do not authorize or APPLY. Stop with the
   single exact `nextCommand` returned by inspection.
10. For an explicit `--repair`, require `kind: repair_ready`, run its exact
    `nextCommand`, then resume APPLY and fresh VERIFY. For
    `repair_recovery_required`, run only that command. Never authorize from a
    normal invocation.

Only invoke the four named Higurashi subagents. Delegation is one level deep.
Use CodeGraph before broad filesystem search for structure, call flow,
dependencies, or impact. Do not silently substitute broad glob, grep, or file
reading when CodeGraph is available.

Report the final durable status and exactly one executable next command when
blocked. Never recommend committing a blocked candidate. Never commit or push.
